  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts, and it passes the typecheck. I couldn't run it or view a frame, so the look, the frame rate and whether the camera moves are untested.\n\n- **Islands:** There are 10, ranging from radius 8 to 30. Each is a procedurally built, flat-shaded rock mesh that tapers to a point, with a green grass top. Each has 3–8 trees (pines or round canopies) and 2–4 glowing crystals. Each bobs on its own speed and phase, and all except the largest also tilt slightly.\n- **Sky:** A custom-shader dome gives an orange-to-pink-to-purple gradient with a large low sun and glow. A fixed-direction warm key light, a pink fill and a hemisphere light light the islands, with a little bloom.\n- **Haze:** Scene fog is lavender-blue, and each island's vertex colours and each cloud's colour are also blended toward a pale blue-violet by distance.\n- **Clouds:** There are 14 banks, each made of 6 soft ellipsoids with a transparent shader. They sit at different depths and heights, and nearer ones drift faster. They wrap around at the edges.\n- **Waterfall:** The largest island has a scrolling-stripe translucent ribbon falling from its edge, plus 48 small droplets that accelerate, spread and shrink as they fall. I used meshes instead of `effects.particles` because I couldn't confirm how the particle emitter behaves.\n- **Camera:** It swings ±26° around the island field and rises and falls slightly. The cycle is a seamless 50 seconds, and there is no user input. It is driven each frame through `app.camera.setPose`. The engine source shows `setPose` is a no-op on its stub camera controller. If that stub is what's active, the camera would stay at its starting position.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and one more animation frame.",
